WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.468 --> 00:00:02.836
- One of the reasons I wrote
the book is people are like,

00:00:02.836 --> 00:00:04.070 align:start size:94% position:6%
"How are you so
optimistic about America?"

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:05.005
I'm like, "'Cause
you know that line,

00:00:05.005 --> 00:00:05.839
history isn't repeated, rhymes."

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:07.307
- [Dave] Yeah.

00:00:07.307 --> 00:00:10.543 align:start size:94% position:6%
- We've seen worse people
who had more control,

00:00:10.543 --> 00:00:13.813 align:start size:94% position:6%
who were more intelligent,
and more evil,

00:00:13.813 --> 00:00:15.548
and willing to go further,

00:00:15.548 --> 00:00:19.419 align:start size:97% position:3%
they lost and they lost
relatively quickly and easily.

00:00:19.419 --> 00:00:22.389
So given that it's not at
all ridiculous to absurd

00:00:22.389 --> 00:00:25.525
to believe, excuse me,
that the same situation

00:00:25.525 --> 00:00:27.761 align:start size:84% position:16%
in a different context
can be replicated

00:00:27.761 --> 00:00:29.629 align:start size:84% position:16%
or at least mirrored
at some extent.

00:00:29.629 --> 00:00:32.065
(slow music)

00:00:36.803 --> 00:00:39.639
- All right, we're at the
local studio here in Miami,

00:00:39.639 --> 00:00:43.777
and I am glad to be with my
good friend Michael Malice,

00:00:43.777 --> 00:00:45.311
who has written a book,

00:00:45.311 --> 00:00:48.381 align:start size:88% position:13%
very apropos, my friend,
"The White Pill,"

00:00:48.381 --> 00:00:50.316 align:start size:84% position:16%
which I believe is
all about Betty White,

00:00:50.316 --> 00:00:51.918 align:start size:84% position:16%
who played, of
course, Rose Nylund,

00:00:51.918 --> 00:00:53.887
on "The Golden Girls" in Miami.

00:00:53.887 --> 00:00:56.423
So Betty White, your feelings.

00:00:56.423 --> 00:00:58.625
- It's actually more the
case for white nationalism.

00:00:58.625 --> 00:01:01.261 align:start size:97% position:3%
(Dave laughing) Did you
ever see when she was on ...

00:01:01.261 --> 00:01:03.430 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I was only able to
thumb through it so far.

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:05.932 align:start size:97% position:3%
It was half white nationalism
and half Betty White.

00:01:05.932 --> 00:01:07.834
- It's a very odd combination,

00:01:07.834 --> 00:01:09.669
but I get you there at the end.

00:01:09.669 --> 00:01:11.838
You gotta start
back in St. Olaf.

00:01:11.838 --> 00:01:14.407
- So I kid you not, I mean,
when I first saw the cover,

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:15.642
whenever you released it,

00:01:15.642 --> 00:01:17.177
maybe six months ago as
the teaser, whatever,

00:01:17.177 --> 00:01:18.445
I see this cover.

00:01:18.445 --> 00:01:19.913
The book is absolutely
beautiful, by the way.

00:01:19.913 --> 00:01:21.915 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I wanna talk about
the publishing part of it.

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:23.516 align:start size:84% position:16%
But I see this cover,
"The White Pill"

00:01:23.516 --> 00:01:25.285
and there's this
sort of sitcomy,

00:01:25.285 --> 00:01:27.187
the font you guys use reminds me

00:01:27.187 --> 00:01:28.822 align:start size:84% position:16%
of like a '70s
sitcom kind of thing.

00:01:28.822 --> 00:01:30.356
And I'm looking at
these women here,

00:01:30.356 --> 00:01:31.691
and then Betty White,

00:01:31.691 --> 00:01:32.759
I'm thinking this is a whole,

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:34.194
I honestly thought for a moment

00:01:34.194 --> 00:01:34.994 align:start size:94% position:6%
this is a whole book
about "The Golden Girls."

00:01:34.994 --> 00:01:36.229
- Well, it actually-

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:37.764 align:start size:88% position:13%
- So can we just talk
about "The Golden Girls"

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:40.533
which I know you're almost an
expert at the level that I am.

00:01:40.533 --> 00:01:41.534
- I think I'm more of an expert.

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:42.802
- You might be more.

00:01:42.802 --> 00:01:44.370
- Yeah, I think if we
had a "Golden Girls"

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:45.939
trivia competition, I think I
would wipe the floor with you.

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:47.407 align:start size:88% position:13%
- You have some of their
clothes, don't you?

00:01:47.407 --> 00:01:48.641 align:start size:97% position:3%
You wear blanches negligees
at night or something?

00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:50.443 align:start size:84% position:16%
- No, I have Margaret
Thatcher's dress.

00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:51.277 align:start size:75% position:25%
(Dave laughs)
And I'm serious.

00:01:51.277 --> 00:01:52.545
- [Dave] Yeah.

00:01:52.545 --> 00:01:54.114
- And I have two of her
bookcases in my house,

00:01:54.114 --> 00:01:56.683
which as an immigrant is such
a sign of like, I've arrived.

00:01:56.683 --> 00:01:57.517
(Dave laughs)

00:01:57.517 --> 00:01:59.119
And let me tell you,

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:01.488 align:start size:88% position:13%
you do not wanna know
how much it costs to get

00:02:01.488 --> 00:02:04.090 align:start size:94% position:6%
two 10 foot tall bookcases
from London to Austin.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:05.358
It's a lot more expensive

00:02:05.358 --> 00:02:06.893 align:start size:84% position:16%
than the actual
bookcases themselves.

00:02:06.893 --> 00:02:08.595
- Yeah, well, I wanna
talk about Austin too,

00:02:08.595 --> 00:02:10.563
because you have fled New York.

00:02:10.563 --> 00:02:11.564
- Yes.

00:02:11.564 --> 00:02:13.166
- You were a big
time New Yorker.

00:02:13.166 --> 00:02:14.400
You were a Brooklyn guy.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:15.535
You did not wanna
leave, but you did.

00:02:15.535 --> 00:02:16.569
But hold on,

00:02:16.569 --> 00:02:17.937 align:start size:84% position:16%
first with the book.
- Yes.

00:02:17.937 --> 00:02:19.038
- Just some technical stuff.

00:02:19.038 --> 00:02:20.807 align:start size:84% position:16%
So you self-published
this, right?

00:02:20.807 --> 00:02:21.741
- [Michael] Yes.

00:02:21.741 --> 00:02:22.942
- This is a self-published book,

00:02:22.942 --> 00:02:24.110 align:start size:84% position:16%
but a hardcover
released on Amazon.

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:25.411 align:start size:75% position:25%
It's blowing up.
- Yes.

00:02:25.411 --> 00:02:26.713
- You're taking out Mike Pence.

00:02:26.713 --> 00:02:27.947
- Yeah, I'm ...

00:02:27.947 --> 00:02:29.782 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Like January 6th style
according to you, no?

00:02:29.782 --> 00:02:31.017
- No, I'm actually
doing it for real.

00:02:31.017 --> 00:02:33.052
- Yeah (laughs),
oh, that was good.

00:02:34.487 --> 00:02:37.223
- I am delighted that we
are at a point in time ...

00:02:37.223 --> 00:02:38.424
When you and I were kids.

00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:39.893
- [Dave] Yeah.

00:02:39.893 --> 00:02:41.161
- And people listen to
this when they were kids.

00:02:41.161 --> 00:02:42.762
If you put out your own
book, you're a crackpot.

00:02:42.762 --> 00:02:43.930
And in fact-

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:45.098
- Yeah, you're a
complete nutbag.

00:02:45.098 --> 00:02:46.299 align:start size:84% position:16%
- You're a complete
nutbag, right?

00:02:46.299 --> 00:02:47.600 align:start size:84% position:16%
And in fact, I have
a little hobby,

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:48.801
sometimes I'll get these
old self-published books

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:50.336 align:start size:88% position:13%
from like '60s and
'70s, and you read them,

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:51.504
and you think there's gonna
be some nuggets in there,

00:02:51.504 --> 00:02:52.772
and it's really, really bad.

00:02:52.772 --> 00:02:54.474 align:start size:81% position:19%
And I have one
book I just bought

00:02:54.474 --> 00:02:55.842
called "Belial:
The Rebel Angel."

00:02:55.842 --> 00:02:57.310 align:start size:84% position:16%
There's no references
to it online.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:59.078 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's the guy who
meets this angel in prison

00:02:59.078 --> 00:03:01.281 align:start size:94% position:6%
and tells him about,
thanks to quantum physics,

00:03:01.281 --> 00:03:02.749
all races are the same.

00:03:02.749 --> 00:03:04.551
It's just, I'm making it
sound much more interesting

00:03:04.551 --> 00:03:06.052
than it is.

00:03:06.052 --> 00:03:09.522
But the fact that I can be
competitive with Mike Pence,

00:03:09.522 --> 00:03:13.459
Obama, all these other
major household names,

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:14.527
when I'm nowhere
near their level

00:03:14.527 --> 00:03:15.762
in terms of name recognition,

00:03:15.762 --> 00:03:17.630 align:start size:81% position:19%
just speaks to how
much publishing

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:19.766
has changed in recent years.

00:03:19.766 --> 00:03:21.601 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that in and of
itself is a white pill

00:03:21.601 --> 00:03:24.804 align:start size:97% position:3%
because for over a century,
if you want to get-

00:03:24.804 --> 00:03:26.472
- Which again, "White Pill.

00:03:26.472 --> 00:03:28.141 align:start size:97% position:3%
- [Michael] whitepillbook.com.
- I see what you did there.

00:03:28.141 --> 00:03:29.609
You said the title of
the book in a sentence.

00:03:29.609 --> 00:03:32.045 align:start size:97% position:3%
- No, but I mean, I'm speaking
to what the white pill is,

00:03:32.045 --> 00:03:33.246
as an expression of hope.

00:03:33.246 --> 00:03:36.316 align:start size:84% position:16%
So for a century,
you had, if you want,

00:03:36.316 --> 00:03:38.685 align:start size:84% position:16%
you couldn't have this
show 20 years ago.

00:03:38.685 --> 00:03:39.886
- No.

00:03:39.886 --> 00:03:40.820
- I mean maybe you
could have it on PBS,

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:41.955
maybe you could have it on Fox,

00:03:41.955 --> 00:03:43.456
maybe let's say 30 years ago.

00:03:43.456 --> 00:03:46.593 align:start size:97% position:3%
But in terms of publishing,
you've got a handful

00:03:46.593 --> 00:03:48.561
of major publishers
which are shrinking.

00:03:48.561 --> 00:03:51.097 align:start size:94% position:6%
They're trying to buy each
other out all the time.

00:03:51.097 --> 00:03:53.433 align:start size:94% position:6%
And if you don't get a
book through one of those,

00:03:53.433 --> 00:03:55.668
all these other outlets
pretend you don't exist.

00:03:55.668 --> 00:03:56.936
Now, that is still the case.

00:03:56.936 --> 00:03:58.638
The New York Times will
pretend I don't exist,

00:03:58.638 --> 00:04:00.039 align:start size:84% position:16%
and "Politico"
pretend I don't exist.

00:04:00.039 --> 00:04:02.642
Sure, but in terms of
me reaching an audience

00:04:02.642 --> 00:04:04.377
and telling a message,

00:04:04.377 --> 00:04:06.579
not only do I not have to
go through a gatekeeper,

00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:08.214 align:start size:88% position:13%
I also don't have to
waste a year of my life.

00:04:08.214 --> 00:04:11.017
Because as you know, if
you get a book deal today

00:04:11.017 --> 00:04:12.885
on like January 1st, 2023,

00:04:12.885 --> 00:04:14.921 align:start size:88% position:13%
it's probably gonna come
out January 1st, 2025

00:04:14.921 --> 00:04:15.755
at the earliest.

00:04:15.755 --> 00:04:16.689
Whereas with me,

00:04:16.689 --> 00:04:17.957
I had it done on a Monday,

00:04:17.957 --> 00:04:20.793
and I can have it out
within a week or two.

00:04:20.793 --> 00:04:22.695 align:start size:97% position:3%
- That was one of the things
that I could not believe

00:04:22.695 --> 00:04:24.130
when I did two books.

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:25.498 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I could not believe.
- Yes.

00:04:25.498 --> 00:04:26.733
- I was like, wait a minute,

00:04:26.733 --> 00:04:28.601
if I write these things
in a couple of months,

00:04:28.601 --> 00:04:30.937 align:start size:94% position:6%
the first one I wrote
basically in three months,

00:04:30.937 --> 00:04:32.705
I was like, I still
have to wait a year.

00:04:32.705 --> 00:04:33.940
- Yeah.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:36.075 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And then suddenly
when after that delay,

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:37.710
it's like, well, I've moved on

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:39.345
from some of those
ideas at this point.

00:04:39.345 --> 00:04:40.546
- Of course.

00:04:40.546 --> 00:04:41.481
- I try to write
something timeless,

00:04:41.481 --> 00:04:42.649
but things do move forward,

00:04:42.649 --> 00:04:44.117 align:start size:84% position:16%
and you're like,
man, this is archaic.

00:04:44.117 --> 00:04:46.552
- Yeah, and publishers complain

00:04:46.552 --> 00:04:50.056
that we're consolidating
and we're losing cloud,

00:04:50.056 --> 00:04:51.424 align:start size:84% position:16%
and what are we
gonna do about this?

00:04:51.424 --> 00:04:54.227 align:start position:0%
But there's not even a
hypothetical mechanism for them

00:04:54.227 --> 00:04:55.161
to turn the ship around.

00:04:55.161 --> 00:04:56.296
And that's a great thing.

00:04:56.296 --> 00:04:57.797
Similarly in journalism,

00:04:57.797 --> 00:04:59.365
wouldn't it be a wonderful
thing if The New York Times

00:04:59.365 --> 00:05:00.600
wasn't The New York Times.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.402 align:start size:88% position:13%
If The New York Times
was not in that position

00:05:02.402 --> 00:05:05.705
to be the officiate and the
dictator of public opinion

00:05:05.705 --> 00:05:07.173
for a large swath of population?

00:05:07.173 --> 00:05:08.708
I think it certainly would be.

00:05:08.708 --> 00:05:10.677
- Yeah, do you think it's fair
to say that it's not anymore?

00:05:10.677 --> 00:05:13.179 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, I think something
does feel like it's changing.

00:05:13.179 --> 00:05:14.647
It's been changing
for a long time,

00:05:14.647 --> 00:05:16.883 align:start position:0%
but I almost think we're on
the other side where it's like,

00:05:16.883 --> 00:05:18.818 align:start size:88% position:13%
if you were sitting next
to somebody at a bar

00:05:18.818 --> 00:05:20.286
and they were reading
"The New York Times,"

00:05:20.286 --> 00:05:22.322 align:start size:84% position:16%
if that's still a
thing that people do,

00:05:22.322 --> 00:05:23.289
is that a thing that people do?

00:05:23.289 --> 00:05:24.857
Probably not-
- At bars?

00:05:24.857 --> 00:05:27.660
- Or at a coffee shop. If
you were at a coffee shop

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:29.095 align:start size:88% position:13%
and somebody was reading
The New York Times,

00:05:29.095 --> 00:05:30.396
I would be looking at them like,

00:05:30.396 --> 00:05:33.800 align:start size:97% position:3%
man, that is an ill-informed,
confused person.

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:37.036
I think that has now sort
of gained more momentum

00:05:37.036 --> 00:05:38.571 align:start size:81% position:19%
than the person
reading The Times.

00:05:38.571 --> 00:05:41.040
- Or you would think, oh,
this person is posturing.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.575 align:start size:84% position:16%
They want you to see-
- Yes.

00:05:42.575 --> 00:05:43.843 align:start size:97% position:3%
- that I'm sitting here
reading The New York Times.

00:05:43.843 --> 00:05:45.278
- With their very
expensive coffee.

00:05:45.278 --> 00:05:49.282 align:start position:0%
- Yes, so I think with a certain
segment of the population,

00:05:49.282 --> 00:05:50.983 align:start size:84% position:16%
there were polls taken
the last few years,

00:05:50.983 --> 00:05:54.053
and in terms of trust of
corporate media outlets,

00:05:54.053 --> 00:05:55.388
just like The New York Times,

00:05:55.388 --> 00:05:57.824 align:start size:84% position:16%
and with Independents
and Republicans,

00:05:57.824 --> 00:05:59.292
the number has collapsed.

00:05:59.292 --> 00:06:02.061 align:start size:97% position:3%
And once you lose trust, it's
really hard to regain it.

00:06:02.061 --> 00:06:03.730
Someone's cheated
on their spouse,

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:05.431
you're never gonna look at
them the same way again.

00:06:05.431 --> 00:06:07.066
'Cause you are, that person
has always been a cheater,

00:06:07.066 --> 00:06:08.368 align:start size:84% position:16%
even if they have
learned their lesson.

00:06:08.368 --> 00:06:09.902 align:start size:84% position:16%
Are you really
ever gonna be sure?

00:06:09.902 --> 00:06:11.371
And when you have
these organizations

00:06:11.371 --> 00:06:14.474 align:start position:0%
that just have constant
propaganda, so on and so forth.

00:06:14.474 --> 00:06:15.942
But with the Democrats,

00:06:15.942 --> 00:06:18.177
their actual trust in corporate
media has actually increased

00:06:18.177 --> 00:06:19.212
over the last few years.

00:06:19.212 --> 00:06:20.413 align:start size:75% position:25%
So what was.
- Is that right?

00:06:20.413 --> 00:06:21.681 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yes, so we're seeing
this bifurcation.

00:06:21.681 --> 00:06:22.882
- Oh, good God, yeah.

00:06:22.882 --> 00:06:24.083 align:start size:84% position:16%
- But if The New York
Times wants to be

00:06:24.083 --> 00:06:26.652 align:start size:88% position:13%
the official house organ
as you would put it,

00:06:26.652 --> 00:06:28.821
the left, that's fine.

00:06:28.821 --> 00:06:31.324
People have much more
of an issue with CNN,

00:06:31.324 --> 00:06:33.693
or at least CNN until the
recent heads were rolling,

00:06:33.693 --> 00:06:35.128
than they had been with MSNBC.

00:06:35.128 --> 00:06:37.397 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yes.
- Maddow was a leftist.

00:06:37.397 --> 00:06:38.564
Chris Hayes was a leftist.

00:06:38.564 --> 00:06:39.832 align:start size:84% position:16%
They have their
leftist point of view.

00:06:39.832 --> 00:06:41.267 align:start size:84% position:16%
Go nuts, speak your
leftist point of view.

00:06:41.267 --> 00:06:42.602
Fox has their right
wing point of view.

00:06:42.602 --> 00:06:44.137
Tucker, go nuts.

00:06:44.137 --> 00:06:45.304
Sean Hannity, we know what
Sean Hannity is gonna say.

00:06:45.304 --> 00:06:47.440
CNN like The New York Times

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:51.644 align:start size:97% position:3%
has this air of
objectivity and neutrality,

00:06:51.644 --> 00:06:53.012
but when push comes to shove,

00:06:53.012 --> 00:06:55.214 align:start size:88% position:13%
they will always fall
the inside of hard left.

00:06:55.214 --> 00:06:58.451
And I think that disingenuous
is what the issue people have

00:06:58.451 --> 00:07:01.487 align:start size:97% position:3%
with both The Times and
formerly CNN decreasingly so.

00:07:01.487 --> 00:07:02.688
- So it's kind of
a beautiful thing

00:07:02.688 --> 00:07:04.223
across industries right now,

00:07:04.223 --> 00:07:06.859
because not only are you able
to get this out independently

00:07:06.859 --> 00:07:08.728
and it's selling
incredibly well,

00:07:08.728 --> 00:07:10.129
that you were telling me
right before you started,

00:07:10.129 --> 00:07:11.631 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, basically
because it's hardcover,

00:07:11.631 --> 00:07:14.000
they can't even catch up with
the printing yet, which is,

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:15.635 align:start size:88% position:13%
I tried to buy an extra
copy this morning.

00:07:15.635 --> 00:07:17.870
It was literally two weeks
till you could even get it.

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:19.405
So it's doing really well.

00:07:19.405 --> 00:07:21.474
We happen to be sitting in
a studio of a tech company

00:07:21.474 --> 00:07:23.342 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I started that
you're huge on,

00:07:23.342 --> 00:07:25.044
Locals, malice.locals.com.

00:07:25.044 --> 00:07:27.313 align:start size:94% position:6%
We've started to actually
build the things.

00:07:27.313 --> 00:07:29.682
I think there was this idea
for the last couple years

00:07:29.682 --> 00:07:32.552 align:start position:0%
that republicans, conservatives,
anyone on the right,

00:07:32.552 --> 00:07:34.987
whatever, hell we all
are at this point.

00:07:34.987 --> 00:07:36.556 align:start size:94% position:6%
We were just gonna
complain about everything.

00:07:36.556 --> 00:07:38.991
But it does seem
like it's turning.

00:07:38.991 --> 00:07:40.526 align:start size:88% position:13%
- There were two
models for this, right?

00:07:40.526 --> 00:07:43.896
So back in the late '70s, I
believe it was early '80s,

00:07:43.896 --> 00:07:46.332 align:start size:84% position:16%
Roger Ailes was
trying to start Fox,

00:07:46.332 --> 00:07:48.100
and they wanted him to be like,

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:50.136
"Okay, we want you to be
competitive with CNN."

00:07:50.136 --> 00:07:52.138
He goes, "I wanna beat CNN."

00:07:52.138 --> 00:07:54.574
But the other model was
for long time people,

00:07:54.574 --> 00:07:56.409
I remember this in
the early 2000s,

00:07:56.409 --> 00:07:59.412
people were like, we need to
have a conservative daily show.

00:07:59.412 --> 00:08:01.047
And I don't think Locals

00:08:01.047 --> 00:08:03.349 align:start size:84% position:16%
is the right wing
version of anything.

00:08:03.349 --> 00:08:04.584
- No.

00:08:04.584 --> 00:08:05.818
- It's not trying to
parallel something

00:08:05.818 --> 00:08:06.752 align:start size:84% position:16%
that the left has
done unsuccessfully.

00:08:06.752 --> 00:08:08.354
So I think the way to succeed,

00:08:08.354 --> 00:08:09.989 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I don't think this
is necessarily the
right versus left,

00:08:09.989 --> 00:08:11.991
is do you want to ape,

00:08:11.991 --> 00:08:13.459 align:start size:84% position:16%
you wanna be like "How
I Met Your Father,"

00:08:13.459 --> 00:08:15.294 align:start size:94% position:6%
or do you wanna do
something that's original

00:08:15.294 --> 00:08:17.063
that you can't get elsewhere?

00:08:17.063 --> 00:08:18.764
And by doing it in that way,

00:08:18.764 --> 00:08:20.500 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think that creates
new audiences.

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:23.169 align:start size:94% position:6%
And when you have
freshness and innovation,

00:08:23.169 --> 00:08:24.670
that's an apolitical thing.

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:26.906
And historically that has
been a function of leftism.

00:08:26.906 --> 00:08:28.341
Leftism is very
highly correlated

00:08:28.341 --> 00:08:30.810
with innovation, with artistry.

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:33.713
And it's only in recent years
that you have organizations

00:08:33.713 --> 00:08:36.082
that can in some sense be
identified as conservative,

00:08:36.082 --> 00:08:37.817
rightly in whatever
you want to call it,

00:08:37.817 --> 00:08:41.521
that have been creating
things that are original,

00:08:41.521 --> 00:08:45.224
or modeling culture.

00:08:45.224 --> 00:08:47.193 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Is that the craziest
cultural shift

00:08:47.193 --> 00:08:48.594
you think we've had that,

00:08:48.594 --> 00:08:50.496 align:start position:0%
'cause the lefties were supposed
to be the creative ones.

00:08:50.496 --> 00:08:52.398
That's generally
how it always was.

00:08:52.398 --> 00:08:53.766 align:start size:84% position:16%
The openness, right?
- Right.

00:08:53.766 --> 00:08:55.234
- The conscientiousness,

00:08:55.234 --> 00:08:57.169
you're gonna create great music
and art and stuff like that.

00:08:57.169 --> 00:08:59.105
Now they've become
the dogmatic ones,

00:08:59.105 --> 00:09:02.742 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you have suddenly
conservatives creating things.

00:09:02.742 --> 00:09:05.411
This is like a massive
psychological shift.

00:09:05.411 --> 00:09:08.281 align:start size:81% position:19%
- I don't think
in modern history,

00:09:08.281 --> 00:09:10.283
meaning let's say
post-Civil war,

00:09:10.283 --> 00:09:13.519
has there ever been red states

00:09:13.519 --> 00:09:15.421
that are the centers
of culture, right?

00:09:15.421 --> 00:09:16.856 align:start size:81% position:19%
In our childhood-
- Right.

00:09:16.856 --> 00:09:18.591 align:start size:84% position:16%
- it would be Boston.
- Yeah.

00:09:18.591 --> 00:09:20.159
Which is now kind
of not a thing.

00:09:20.159 --> 00:09:21.394
- Yep.

00:09:21.394 --> 00:09:22.895
- Chicago was major
when we were kids.

00:09:22.895 --> 00:09:24.964
Now it's not a thing at all
in terms of creating culture,

00:09:24.964 --> 00:09:26.098
great food and so
on and so forth.

00:09:26.098 --> 00:09:27.567
But it's not what Chicago was,

00:09:27.567 --> 00:09:28.768
when Oprah and Phil
Donahue were there,

00:09:28.768 --> 00:09:29.569
right.
- Yeah.

00:09:29.569 --> 00:09:30.970
- And New York and LA.

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:33.706 align:start size:84% position:16%
Now, Austin and Miami
are both becoming

00:09:33.706 --> 00:09:35.107
in their own way, cultural hubs.

00:09:35.107 --> 00:09:36.442 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yep.
- I can't think of any ...

00:09:36.442 --> 00:09:39.211 align:start size:84% position:16%
Seattle was a cultural
hub in a small way.

00:09:39.211 --> 00:09:42.181 align:start size:81% position:19%
Portland, Detroit,
none of these.

00:09:42.181 --> 00:09:44.650
But now to have
a red state with,

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:46.919
Austin is a blue
city very much so.

00:09:46.919 --> 00:09:48.688
Miami I think has a
Republican governor,

00:09:48.688 --> 00:09:49.755
if I'm not mistaken.

00:09:49.755 --> 00:09:51.123
- Republican mayor and governor.

00:09:51.123 --> 00:09:53.292 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Excuse me, Republican
mayor, I meant.

00:09:53.292 --> 00:09:56.295 align:start size:97% position:3%
This is historically an
America culture unprecedented.

00:09:56.295 --> 00:09:57.863
And I don't know what it means,

00:09:57.863 --> 00:10:01.267
but I am looking forward to
already being the big fish

00:10:01.267 --> 00:10:02.501
in the small pond of Austin.

00:10:02.501 --> 00:10:03.736
- Yeah, so let's talk about that

00:10:03.736 --> 00:10:05.471
because I haven't had
you on for a while.

00:10:05.471 --> 00:10:07.373
I still lived in that crazy LA.

00:10:07.373 --> 00:10:09.108
And you still lived in
that crazy Brooklyn.

00:10:09.108 --> 00:10:11.911
So we both made moves
since we last sat down

00:10:11.911 --> 00:10:13.312
about a year and a half ago.

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:16.882
I take it you are
happier in Texas

00:10:16.882 --> 00:10:18.351
than you were in New York.

00:10:18.351 --> 00:10:21.287
- I didn't remember how,

00:10:21.287 --> 00:10:23.923
I didn't realize how lonely

00:10:23.923 --> 00:10:25.992
New York had gotten
towards the end.

00:10:25.992 --> 00:10:26.926
- [Dave] Yeah.

00:10:26.926 --> 00:10:28.060
- It was like "Survivor."

00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:30.262
One by one of my friends.

00:10:30.262 --> 00:10:31.764
I don't mean people
I've seen at a party,

00:10:31.764 --> 00:10:34.100
just people I talk to on
regular basis moved away.

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:35.768
You're playing poker and
some went like, "I'm out."

00:10:35.768 --> 00:10:37.303
- Yeah.
- I fold.

00:10:37.303 --> 00:10:39.605
I have just a couple of
friends left in New York.

00:10:39.605 --> 00:10:41.641 align:start size:84% position:16%
The majority of
them move to Austin.

00:10:41.641 --> 00:10:43.643
And to be able to
have a social group

00:10:43.643 --> 00:10:45.544
is something that is wonderful.

00:10:45.544 --> 00:10:47.213
And New Yorkers are told a lie.

00:10:47.213 --> 00:10:49.248 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I believed this
my entire life,

00:10:49.248 --> 00:10:51.250
which is, "These people
who move out in New York,

00:10:51.250 --> 00:10:53.419 align:start size:84% position:16%
oh, they're weak,
they can't hack it."

00:10:53.419 --> 00:10:54.854 align:start size:84% position:16%
And at a certain
point it does become

00:10:54.854 --> 00:10:56.188 align:start size:84% position:16%
like "Atlas Shrugged"
Ayn Rand's book.

00:10:56.188 --> 00:10:58.791
It's like why am I
carrying this burden?

00:10:58.791 --> 00:11:00.926
What is my upside here, so
I could stand up and say,

00:11:00.926 --> 00:11:02.361
"Look how tough I
am a New Yorker."

00:11:02.361 --> 00:11:03.963
No one's ever gonna
perceive me as tough.

00:11:03.963 --> 00:11:06.132 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well there was a time
where that had value, right?

00:11:06.132 --> 00:11:07.400
- [Michael] Sure.

00:11:07.400 --> 00:11:08.334
- There was a time
when the toughness

00:11:08.334 --> 00:11:09.568
and the culture was there,

00:11:09.568 --> 00:11:10.569
but it just simply
isn't there anymore.

00:11:10.569 --> 00:11:12.104
- I went back over the summer,

00:11:12.104 --> 00:11:13.339
I hadn't been there
for a whole year

00:11:13.339 --> 00:11:14.473
'cause Tim Pool had
put up a billboard

00:11:14.473 --> 00:11:15.708
of me in Times Square.

00:11:15.708 --> 00:11:16.976
I'm like, "I gotta see
some person, right?"

00:11:16.976 --> 00:11:18.244
This is bucket list stuff.

00:11:18.244 --> 00:11:19.812
- Literally put you
up in Times Square,

00:11:19.812 --> 00:11:21.647
your face Tim cast.

00:11:21.647 --> 00:11:24.850
- Yeah, looking down at the
masses as is my destiny.

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:29.855
And I was so angered to
see that all the places,

00:11:31.023 --> 00:11:31.957
because obviously
a lot of places

00:11:31.957 --> 00:11:33.726
I adored didn't survive COVID.

00:11:33.726 --> 00:11:35.594
You would think after a
year there'd be healing,

00:11:35.594 --> 00:11:37.830 align:start size:94% position:6%
people would be returning,
you know what I mean?

00:11:37.830 --> 00:11:39.065
I would say like a quarter

00:11:39.065 --> 00:11:40.533
of the storefronts
were unoccupied.

00:11:40.533 --> 00:11:43.502 align:start size:84% position:16%
There was not this
enthusiasm in the air.

00:11:43.502 --> 00:11:46.272 align:start size:97% position:3%
There was really no place that
I wanted to go necessarily.

00:11:46.272 --> 00:11:50.376
It is not fertile soil to
have a renaissance anymore.

00:11:50.376 --> 00:11:52.611
And that is something
that even pre Giuliani,

00:11:52.611 --> 00:11:54.680 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'd never experienced
in my lifetime.

00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:58.050
New York's crime era,
people don't realize,

00:11:58.050 --> 00:11:59.518
it was an opportunity

00:11:59.518 --> 00:12:01.353 align:start size:81% position:19%
because if you
were a poor artist

00:12:01.353 --> 00:12:03.355
who can handle living
in dangerous streets,

00:12:03.355 --> 00:12:04.890
you could still get an apartment

00:12:04.890 --> 00:12:07.259
and you could still go there
and create and make a scene.

00:12:07.259 --> 00:12:09.695
And the rents are
too high for that.

00:12:09.695 --> 00:12:12.031
And everything else
is too difficult.

00:12:12.031 --> 00:12:15.201 align:start size:97% position:3%
You're not going to be a
cultural magnet as you were,

00:12:15.201 --> 00:12:17.169
2030 or 30 years
ago, in the '70s

00:12:17.169 --> 00:12:18.637
when the punk scene was there.

00:12:18.637 --> 00:12:20.272
- So what do you think
happens to New York City?

00:12:20.272 --> 00:12:22.141 align:start size:88% position:13%
Because to me it has
not hit rock bottom yet.

00:12:22.141 --> 00:12:23.642 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean-
- I agree with you.

00:12:23.642 --> 00:12:24.677
- The times that I've been
there in the last two years,

00:12:24.677 --> 00:12:27.113
it's more depressing each time.

00:12:27.113 --> 00:12:28.647 align:start size:84% position:16%
There are no people
doing business.

00:12:28.647 --> 00:12:29.615
That's what I always noticed.

00:12:29.615 --> 00:12:31.584
Even in Midtown.

00:12:31.584 --> 00:12:32.785
So I've gone for "Gutfeld"

00:12:32.785 --> 00:12:34.286 align:start size:84% position:16%
so Fox is right in
the middle of Midtown.

00:12:34.286 --> 00:12:35.855
It's supposed to
be people in suits.

00:12:35.855 --> 00:12:37.356
That's what you used to see,

00:12:37.356 --> 00:12:40.326
groups of guys in suits going
to lunch and doing business.

00:12:40.326 --> 00:12:43.162 align:start size:94% position:6%
Now it's just people in
hoodies all walking alone.

00:12:43.162 --> 00:12:45.030 align:start size:84% position:16%
Everyone looks like
they're up to no good.

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:46.232
And the whole place
smells like weed.

00:12:46.232 --> 00:12:48.567 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I'm not against
weed as a general rule,

00:12:48.567 --> 00:12:51.270
and I know you're certainly not
a against making it illegal.

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:52.238
- Right.

00:12:52.238 --> 00:12:54.573
- But your business center

00:12:54.573 --> 00:12:56.175 align:start size:81% position:19%
shouldn't smell
like weed all day.

00:12:56.175 --> 00:12:58.110
- It's kind of scary
that having my face

00:12:58.110 --> 00:12:59.745 align:start size:81% position:19%
in Times Square
isn't rock bottom.

00:12:59.745 --> 00:13:02.381
I would've thought this
is as low as we get.

00:13:02.381 --> 00:13:04.283 align:start size:84% position:16%
But apparently the
basement has a seller.

00:13:04.283 --> 00:13:05.985
- [Dave] Yeah.

00:13:05.985 --> 00:13:09.922
- And the crime is
just something that
I remember as a kid,

00:13:09.922 --> 00:13:12.057
kind of in the background
'cause I was much younger.

00:13:12.057 --> 00:13:13.793 align:start size:84% position:16%
But to have that
kind of sense of ...

00:13:13.793 --> 00:13:16.195 align:start size:84% position:16%
the thing that I think
is the worst part

00:13:16.195 --> 00:13:19.365 align:start size:84% position:16%
is there doesn't
seem to be any will-

00:13:19.365 --> 00:13:20.299
- [Dave] Yeah.

00:13:20.299 --> 00:13:21.534
- Or plan to turn it around.

00:13:21.534 --> 00:13:24.136
Now, if Mayor Adams was
like, let's try this,

00:13:24.136 --> 00:13:25.070
let's try that,

00:13:25.070 --> 00:13:26.806
or Governor Hochul was saying,

00:13:26.806 --> 00:13:28.574
this is our plan to kind of ...

00:13:28.574 --> 00:13:29.809 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I have a lot of faith
in her by the way.

00:13:29.809 --> 00:13:31.177 align:start size:84% position:16%
- But they're not even
identifying issues.

00:13:31.177 --> 00:13:32.678
They're just kind of being like,

00:13:32.678 --> 00:13:34.213
"Oh, you're just making this
up for propaganda purpose.

00:13:34.213 --> 00:13:35.481
New York is safer than ever."

00:13:35.481 --> 00:13:37.416
It's just like, okay, if
people don't feel safe,

00:13:37.416 --> 00:13:38.717 align:start size:84% position:16%
that in and of
itself is a problem.

00:13:38.717 --> 00:13:40.920 align:start size:97% position:3%
So why are they misperceiving
in your opinion,

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:42.121
this lack of safety?

00:13:42.121 --> 00:13:43.122 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So a guy like
you who strikes me,

00:13:43.122 --> 00:13:44.456
you're such a New Yorker.

00:13:44.456 --> 00:13:45.758 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like you are (speaking
simultaneously)

00:13:45.758 --> 00:13:47.526
in the best sense of
that, I mean that.

00:13:47.526 --> 00:13:48.828
So you go to Texas.

00:13:48.828 --> 00:13:50.262
Now, you're in Austin,

00:13:50.262 --> 00:13:52.398 align:start size:94% position:6%
which obviously is a
blue city in a red state.

00:13:53.566 --> 00:13:56.068
Culture shock or did
you just get there

00:13:56.068 --> 00:13:57.570
and you're good to go?

00:13:57.570 --> 00:13:59.171
- Oh, well it's kind of cute
because some people come at me,

00:13:59.171 --> 00:14:00.940
they're like, you moved to
the San Francisco of Texas.

00:14:00.940 --> 00:14:03.275 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm like, yeah, the
keyword here is of Texas.

00:14:03.275 --> 00:14:05.377 align:start size:84% position:16%
This is not needles
on the street.

00:14:05.377 --> 00:14:06.879
- [Dave] Right.

00:14:06.879 --> 00:14:09.582
- Like complete Chesa Boudin,
District Attorney situation.

00:14:09.582 --> 00:14:11.851 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's still very
Texan in its own way.

00:14:11.851 --> 00:14:14.820
- But there is a pretty
decent homeless problem.

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:16.121
- No, we had a referendum.

00:14:16.121 --> 00:14:17.523
They cleaned them up.

00:14:17.523 --> 00:14:18.791 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Is that right? Okay, good.
- They took 'em ...

00:14:18.791 --> 00:14:20.626
It's not like, no, it's
not like back in LA, no.

00:14:20.626 --> 00:14:21.894
They used to be like LA.

00:14:21.894 --> 00:14:23.162
- Yeah.

00:14:23.162 --> 00:14:24.096
- Like the entire
street was tense.

00:14:24.096 --> 00:14:25.865
I don't know where they went.

00:14:25.865 --> 00:14:28.100 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't really
care at this point,

00:14:28.100 --> 00:14:30.202
but I mean, 'cause you heard,
it was really, really bad.

00:14:30.202 --> 00:14:32.705
You could, I visited during
COVID, I was doing Rogan,

00:14:32.705 --> 00:14:34.306
and I wanted to
go get ice cream,

00:14:34.306 --> 00:14:35.608
and I had take an
Uber a few blocks

00:14:35.608 --> 00:14:37.076 align:start size:88% position:13%
'cause the streets were
completely deserted

00:14:37.076 --> 00:14:39.144
and the only people on the
streets were literal junkies.

00:14:39.144 --> 00:14:41.280
I'm like, this is not
a good look for me.

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:42.915
This is not gonna
end well for me.

00:14:42.915 --> 00:14:45.084
But now it's just absolutely,

00:14:45.084 --> 00:14:46.485
I'm just very, very happy.

00:14:46.485 --> 00:14:48.754
The fact that I can own
a gun in my own home

00:14:48.754 --> 00:14:51.090
is just wonderfully reassuring.

00:14:51.090 --> 00:14:52.791
'Cause in New York,
I have cool stuff.

00:14:52.791 --> 00:14:54.026
I put it Instagram sometimes.

00:14:54.026 --> 00:14:55.694
If someone breaks into my house,

00:14:55.694 --> 00:14:57.363
I can't defend myself
or I'm going to jail.

00:14:57.363 --> 00:14:59.265
They'll be much more likely

00:14:59.265 --> 00:15:01.467 align:start size:97% position:3%
to prosecute someone who
defends their home with a gun

00:15:01.467 --> 00:15:03.102 align:start size:84% position:16%
than someone who
breaks into the house.

00:15:03.102 --> 00:15:06.538 align:start size:84% position:16%
Now, if someone
comes into my house,

00:15:06.538 --> 00:15:08.474
even if they're my friend, I
can shoot 'em and it's Texas,

00:15:08.474 --> 00:15:09.475
and I'll have no repercussions.

00:15:09.475 --> 00:15:10.676
- If they are coming-

00:15:10.676 --> 00:15:12.211 align:start size:81% position:19%
- So come on over,
Dave. (laughs)

00:15:12.211 --> 00:15:13.479
- If they're coming for your
Blanche Devereaux Negligee,

00:15:13.479 --> 00:15:16.715
they are gonna get a
bullet in their ass.

00:15:16.715 --> 00:15:18.484 align:start size:84% position:16%
- It exactly covers
a perky bosom.

00:15:18.484 --> 00:15:22.955
- What is it about Austin that
